# FPGA-Based DTMF Tone Generator and Decoder

**Direct Digital Synthesis + Goertzel Algorithm on the RealDigital Boolean Board (AMD/Xilinx Artix-7)**

## Overview

This project implements a complete DTMF (Dual-Tone Multi-Frequency) codec entirely in digital hardware. DTMF is the signaling standard used in telephone keypads, IVR systems, and ATMs — each key press transmits two simultaneous audio frequencies (one "row" tone, one "column" tone), and a receiver identifies both to recover the original key.

The system implements **both halves** of this protocol on a single FPGA:
- A **tone generator** that synthesizes the correct dual-tone pair for any of the 16 standard DTMF symbols (`0`–`9`, `A`–`D`, `*`, `#`)
- A **tone decoder** that analyzes an incoming audio stream and identifies which key was pressed

This maps directly to core Electronics & Communication Engineering concepts: signal synthesis, digital filtering, and telecom protocol design — implemented as synchronous digital logic rather than in software.

## Key Techniques

| Technique | Where it's used | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Direct Digital Synthesis (DDS)** | Tone generation | Phase-accumulator + sine lookup table generates precise, tunable sine waves digitally — the same principle used in real RF/signal-generation hardware |
| **Goertzel Algorithm** | Tone decoding | An efficient single-frequency energy detector — the same technique used in real DTMF decoder ICs and telecom equipment, more efficient than a full FFT when only a handful of known frequencies need detection |
| **Fixed-point arithmetic** | Both paths | All signal processing is done in fixed-point (no floating point), matching real hardware DSP design constraints |
| **PWM DAC** | Audio output | Converts digital samples to an analog-ish signal for audible output via a standard audio jack |

## Architecture

```
switches[3:0] (key select) ──┐
                              ├─► dtmf_freq_lut ──► DDS(low tone) ──┐
btn_send ─────────────────────┘                                     ├─► SUM ──► pwm_dac ──► AUDIO OUT
                                                DDS(high tone) ──────┘
                                                                            │
                                                                    (shared 8kHz sample stream)
                                                                            │
                                    8× goertzel_block (697,770,852,941,1209,1336,1477,1633 Hz)
                                                        │
                                          argmax(low group) + argmax(high group)
                                                        │
                                              decoded_key[3:0] ──► 7-segment display + status LED
```

A single shared **8 kHz sample clock** drives both the tone generator and the decoder, enabling a fully self-contained digital loopback design with no external microphone required for core functional verification.

### Key Encoding

Standard telephone keypad matrix — `key[3:2]` selects the row (low frequency), `key[1:0]` selects the column (high frequency):

| key[3:0] | Digit | key[3:0] | Digit | key[3:0] | Digit | key[3:0] | Digit |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `0x0` | 1 | `0x4` | 4 | `0x8` | 7 | `0xC` | * |
| `0x1` | 2 | `0x5` | 5 | `0x9` | 8 | `0xD` | 0 |
| `0x2` | 3 | `0x6` | 6 | `0xA` | 9 | `0xE` | # |
| `0x3` | A | `0x7` | B | `0xB` | C | `0xF` | D |

## Repository Structure

```
├── src/
│   ├── sine_lut32.v          # 32-point sine lookup table for DDS
│   ├── phase_acc.v           # DDS phase accumulator
│   ├── dds_channel.v         # Single-tone DDS generator (phase_acc + sine_lut32)
│   ├── sample_clk_gen.v      # 8kHz sample-rate clock divider
│   ├── dtmf_freq_lut.v       # Key code -> DDS frequency word lookup
│   ├── dtmf_tx.v             # Dual-tone generator top level
│   ├── pwm_dac.v             # PWM-based digital-to-analog conversion
│   ├── goertzel_block.v      # Single-frequency Goertzel energy detector
│   ├── dtmf_rx.v             # 8-channel decoder + argmax key recovery
│   ├── seven_seg_hex.v       # 7-segment hex/DTMF-symbol display driver
│   └── dtmf_top.v            # Top-level integration
├── sim/
│   ├── tb_sine_lut32.v
│   ├── tb_phase_acc.v
│   ├── tb_sample_clk_gen.v
│   ├── tb_dds_channel.v
│   ├── tb_dtmf_freq_lut.v
│   ├── tb_dtmf_tx.v
│   ├── tb_pwm_dac.v
│   ├── tb_goertzel_block.v
│   ├── tb_dtmf_rx.v
│   ├── tb_seven_seg_hex.v
│   └── tb_dtmf_top.v         # Full-system testbench (all 16 keys)
└── constraints/
    └── dtmf_top.xdc
```

## Tools

- **Vivado 2025.2** (XSim behavioral simulator, synthesis, implementation)
- **Verilog-2001** (no SystemVerilog-only constructs)
- Target device: `xc7a35tcsg324-1` (AMD/Xilinx Artix-7, RealDigital Boolean Board)

## Results

### Simulation
Full-system testbench (`tb_dtmf_top.v`) verified all 16 standard DTMF symbols decode correctly through the complete digital-loopback pipeline (generator → shared sample stream → decoder):

```
PASS: key 0 decoded correctly
PASS: key 1 decoded correctly
...
PASS: key f decoded correctly
=== Testbench complete ===
```

Individual unit testbenches additionally verify each module (sine table symmetry, DDS frequency accuracy, Goertzel frequency discrimination, display segment mapping, etc.) in isolation.

### Hardware
Deployed and tested on the RealDigital Boolean Board:
- ✅ **7-segment display** — correctly shows the decoded key for all 16 inputs
- ✅ **Status LED** — correctly indicates a valid decode while the send button is held
- 🔧 **Audio output** — decode logic is confirmed correct via the display/LED; the analog audio path (PWM → jack) is still being debugged, currently isolating whether the board's audio jack requires a proper I2S-based audio codec interface rather than raw single-bit PWM

## Design Notes / Known Limitations

- Goertzel coefficients are computed for a 205-sample block at an 8 kHz sample rate (`k = round(205·f/8000)`), matching standard telecom Goertzel implementations for DTMF.
- The design decodes one key at a time (digital loopback); it does not yet decode a live acoustic signal from a microphone.
- PWM audio output assumes the board's audio jack accepts raw PWM; if the board uses a dedicated audio codec IC, a proper I2S transmitter would be needed instead (see Known Limitations above).

